+// --audio-stream-silence on a passthrough track (AudioTrack_keepAlive). The
+// core then leaves the track playing across pauses, seeks and underruns and
+// simply has no audio for the worker. Left alone the track would run dry,
+// the HAL would stop the HDMI bitstream, and a receiver that lost the stream
+// re-locks on resume - a Trinnov takes 5-15 s of silence to do so. Instead
+// the worker keeps the stream going with silence the receiver decodes as the
+// same format:
+//
+// - A raw track (ENCODING_AC3/E_AC3/DTS) gets a pre-encoded silent frame of
+//   its codec, 2.0 or 5.1 after the layout the real frames carry.
+// - The IEC 61937 carrier gets zero words: the padding between bursts is
+//   zero words anyway, and the HAL keeps the port in bitstream mode.
+// - Raw TrueHD and raw DTS-HD have no silent frame; they drop the stream on
+//   pause as before.
+//
+// Silence may only start where a burst ends. ad_spdif queues whole bursts,
+// so reads of one burst (the format's largest until the stream's own is
+// known) end on a boundary even when short, and AudioTrack_trackBurst
+// follows the wire position so that a burst cut short is completed with
+// zeros first. Pacing keeps KA_AHEAD_NS of silence queued ahead of the
+// playback head; a head that stands still gets the track filled instead,
+// since a direct track that stopped starts again only on a full buffer.
+// Audio is paced too, to KA_QUEUE_NS: the core never flushes the track with
+// the option on (ao_reset skips the driver), so whatever is queued at a seek
+// or a pause plays out first. A raw track's byte budget holds over a second
+// of a low-bitrate stream; the pacing keeps the playout under half of that,
+// and still fills the track when the head stands still, as the silence does.
+// Nothing is fed before the track's first real audio, and the track is not
+// started before it either: the core starts the AO at init, a bitstream
+// track started empty sits in AudioFlinger's underrun pause until its first
+// frame, and a buffer of silence there would only delay that frame. start()
+// leaves the track paused and the worker starts it once the first frame is
+// in it (AudioTrack_deferredPlay).
+//
+// A feed step that takes longer than KA_STALL_LOG_NS is logged by name: the
+// cushion is KA_AHEAD_NS, so a stall of that order lets the track run dry,
+// and one seen in the field should say where the time went.
+//
+// The feed is bounded. After --audiotrack-stream-silence-limit seconds of
+// silence in one feed (60 by default, as Kodi's; 0 for no limit; read when
+// the AO opens) the worker stands the track down (AudioTrack_keepAliveStop):
+// it pauses and flushes it as stop() does, so that the output goes to standby
+// and the bitstream stops, and the receiver is not held on a dead signal
+// indefinitely. (The track is kept: an HDMI HAL's one direct output stays
+// this AO's until it closes, as it does across a plain pause.) The monitor
+// has the worker do the same, instead of recreating the track, when the
+// device stops taking the silence (the receiver or the display was turned off
+// during the pause), and the worker does the same when a silence write fails
+// (the track died with the device): nothing is lost by it, where a recreate
+// that fails would end playback while the user is away, and the next audio
+// recovers a dead track the usual way. A track stood down starts primed with
+// its next audio as it did with its first (AudioTrack_deferredPlay), and a
+// receiver that was off re-locks as it would without the option. The monitor
+// requests the stand-down and pauses the track in one step under track_lock;
+// the worker's answer, the stand-down or the resumption, takes that lock, so
+// the pause cannot land after it.
+//
+// A PCM track gets no silence from here: the core pads its reads with
+// silence (ao_read_data), and the track never runs dry. A padded read that
+// copied nothing counts as feeding all the same, so that a device that stops
+// taking the padding stands the track down instead of ending playback, and
+// the next audio starts it again (AudioTrack_keepAlivePad). The limit does
+// not apply to it.
+//
+// Silence counts in written_frames like audio, so the delay the core sees
+// includes it and the audio queued behind it is timed after it: A/V sync
+// holds. The core hands out nothing while paused (read_buffer), so audio in
+// flight at the pause plays out before the silence begins.
+#define KA_AHEAD_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(300)
+#define KA_HEAD_STILL_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(250)
+#define KA_POLL_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(5)
+#define KA_STALL_LOG_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(100)
+// How much audio the worker lets a moving head have queued before it waits
+// for the queue to drain rather than read more: what plays out after a seek
+// or at a pause. Above KA_AHEAD_NS by more than a burst and the lag of the
+// smoothed head the queue is judged from, below the byte budget a raw track
+// holds of a low-bitrate stream (1.28 s of 192 kb/s AC-3).
+#define KA_QUEUE_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(500)
+// A track stood down, or one waiting for its first audio, polls for the
+// core's next audio with waits that double from KA_POLL_NS up to this.
+// Nothing wakes the worker when that audio arrives (a pull driver is not
+// told), so the cap bounds how late the first frame of a title, or the first
+// after a stand-down, is written: the receiver's lock starts with that
+// frame, so a wait here comes on top of it. A shorter cap would have a file
+// that sits paused before its first frame query the track for nothing. A
+// track that gets no silence (a format without any) polls at KA_POLL_NS
+// while it plays, so that its next audio is not late.
+#define KA_IDLE_NS MP_TIME_MS_TO_NS(50)
+
